{"externally_confirmed_facts":["`javax.net.ssl.SSLContext` represents a secure-socket protocol implementation and is a factory for `SSLSocketFactory`, `SSLServerSocketFactory`, and `SSLEngine` objects. Its relevant initialization event is `init(KeyManager[] km, TrustManager[] tm, SecureRandom random)`.","The `SSLContext.init` API permits a null `KeyManager[]`. Its Javadoc states: “The first two parameters either may be null in which case the installed security providers will be searched for the highest priority implementation of the appropriate factory. Likewise, the secure random parameter may be null in which case the default implementation will be used.” Thus, `init(null, tm, random)` is API-valid; the API documentation does not define it as an error condition.","`km` supplies authentication-key sources and `tm` supplies peer-authentication trust decisions. The JSSE Reference Guide documents initializing a newly created `SSLContext` with `init(KeyManager[], TrustManager[], SecureRandom)`. It documents obtaining manager arrays from `KeyManagerFactory` and `TrustManagerFactory`, and also permits applicable `KeyManager` and `TrustManager` interface implementations instead of factory-produced managers.","The official `SSLContext` and JSSE sources reviewed do not call a null `km` argument unsafe or prescribe a mandatory replacement. If an external rule requires explicit factory provenance, the documented factory route is to initialize a `KeyManagerFactory` with applicable key material and provide the resulting `getKeyManagers()` array. That is not an API-wide requirement: the JSSE guide also documents concrete manager implementations.","The documented sources do not support weakening certificate validation, trust management, or hostname verification in response to a key-manager provenance finding. Trust managers are responsible for peer-authentication trust decisions.","The rule’s protocol literal condition is distinct from enabled and negotiated TLS protocols. The JSSE Reference Guide states that an `SSLContext` from `getInstance()` implements at least the requested protocol but may implement others; enabled protocols are controlled on the resulting `SSLSocket`, `SSLServerSocket`, or `SSLEngine`. Therefore, a rule condition on `getInstance("TLSv1.2")` or `getInstance("TLSv1.3")` is not documentation of the ultimately enabled or negotiated protocol. In particular, `getInstance("TLS")` alone does not establish a negotiated legacy protocol.","The reviewed sources do not document a persisted file, object, configuration, or wire-format migration resulting solely from replacing a null `KeyManager[]` with an explicit array. They also provide no dedicated migration procedure for that change. Since key managers select authentication credentials, changed manager/key-material configuration can affect interoperability, but the reviewed documentation does not promise equivalent credential selection between configurations.","`SSLContext` and `KeyManagerFactory` are documented as available since Java 1.4. Current Java SE documentation lists `TLSv1.2` and `TLSv1.3` as required standard `SSLContext` protocols. Oracle’s JDK 11 migration documentation says TLS 1.3 was introduced and enabled by default in JDK 11; this does not establish equivalent TLS 1.3 availability on earlier JDK releases.","The primary CrySL source declares its governed type with the exact clause `SPEC javax.net.ssl.SSLContext`. Its matching event declarations are, verbatim:
```
g1: getInstance(protocol);
g2: getInstance(protocol, _);
Get := g1 | g2;
i1: init(km, tm, random);
Init := i1;
se1: eng = createSSLEngine();
se2: eng = createSSLEngine(_,_);
Engine := se1 | se2;
```
The matching method names are therefore `getInstance`, `init`, and `createSSLEngine`; the rule’s declared receiver/governed type is `javax.net.ssl.SSLContext`.","The CrySL event declarations constrain call arity and bindings: `getInstance(protocol)` binds one argument, while `getInstance(protocol, _)` binds the first and leaves the second unconstrained; `init(km, tm, random)` binds three arguments; `createSSLEngine()` has zero arguments; and `createSSLEngine(_,_)` has two unconstrained arguments. The source does not declare an event for a merely same-named method on an unrelated type. Its `SPEC javax.net.ssl.SSLContext` clause scopes the rule to that API type; analyzer dispatch/resolution mechanics beyond the declared scope are not described by this source.","The CrySL source evaluates the order expression `Get, Init, Engine?`; the constraint `protocol in {"TLSv1.2", "TLSv1.3"}`; and the required predicates `generatedKeyManagers[km]`, `generatedTrustManagers[tm]`, and `randomized[random]`. It also contains the exact forbidden declaration `getDefault() => Get;`. The source establishes postconditions for a generated SSL context after `Init` and a generated SSL engine after `Engine`.","The cited CrySL file does not contain `null`, `KeyManagerFactory`, or “default key managers,” and it does not syntactically test that `km` is non-null. Its relevant condition is `generatedKeyManagers[km]`. Consequently, the reported presentation text that a null array “requests the platform default key managers” and that those managers were “not observed coming from a KeyManagerFactory” is not a literal condition stated in this CrySL source."],"sources":[{"relevance":"Official API contract for `SSLContext`, including its purpose, `init` parameter semantics, null-argument handling, standard protocol names, and API availability.","limitations":"This API page does not define CogniCrypt predicate semantics or detector diagnostic labels. Null-manager behavior is described in terms of provider/factory selection and may require provider- and JDK-specific documentation for runtime details.","claim":"`SSLContext.init` permits null key-manager and trust-manager arrays; providers are searched for an appropriate highest-priority factory. `SSLContext` is a protocol-implementation/factory API.","authority":"official","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/26/docs/api/java.base/javax/net/ssl/SSLContext.html"},{"relevance":"Official JSSE usage guidance for context initialization, manager factories and custom managers, protocol implementation/enabling distinctions, and manager responsibilities.","limitations":"The guide does not prescribe a migration specifically from null manager arrays to explicit arrays, nor does it guarantee equivalent selected credentials across manager configurations.","claim":"A context is initialized using key managers, trust managers, and a secure random source; manager arrays may be factory-created or supplied through applicable interface implementations. Enabled protocols are configured on socket/engine objects rather than inferred solely from the `SSLContext.getInstance` argument.","authority":"official","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/27/security/java-secure-socket-extension-jsse-reference-guide.html"},{"relevance":"Official API documentation for factory-created key managers.","limitations":"It documents the factory API but does not make factory output mandatory for every `SSLContext.init` invocation.","claim":"`KeyManagerFactory` creates key managers based on key material, and `getKeyManagers()` returns the managers.","authority":"official","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/26/docs/api/java.base/javax/net/ssl/KeyManagerFactory.html"},{"relevance":"Official JDK compatibility context for TLS 1.3 availability.","limitations":"This JDK 11 migration material does not characterize every Java vendor/version or define a manager-array migration path.","claim":"JDK 11 introduced TLS 1.3 support and enabled it by default.","authority":"official","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/11/migrate/"},{"relevance":"Primary source for the governed type, event patterns, parameter shapes, order, constraints, predicates, and forbidden call in the reported CrySL rule.","limitations":"The source does not explain analyzer resolution implementation, predicate provenance semantics, compiled-rule behavior, or the presentation-level wording of diagnostics. The URL references the repository’s `master` branch and can change over time.","claim":"The rule is scoped by `SPEC javax.net.ssl.SSLContext`; it declares `getInstance`, `init`, and `createSSLEngine` event patterns; requires the stated protocol and provenance predicates; and declares `getDefault() => Get` forbidden.","authority":"primary","url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/CROSSINGTUD/Crypto-API-Rules/refs/heads/master/JavaCryptographicArchitecture/src/SSLContext.crysl"}],"queries_performed":["Reviewed the supplied primary CrySL-rule URL for the `SSLContext` specification and its matching/event clauses.","Reviewed Oracle Java SE `SSLContext` and `KeyManagerFactory` API documentation cited in the supplied research material.","Reviewed the Oracle JSSE Reference Guide and Oracle JDK 11 migration guide cited in the supplied research material."],"externally_unresolved_questions":["Which JDK release, JSSE provider, and provider configuration apply at runtime? The reviewed API sources do not fully resolve provider-specific behavior behind a null `KeyManager[]` in a particular environment.","What exact CrySL analyzer version, compiled rule artifact, and predicate implementation generated the detector wording? The source rule itself does not define the terms “null,” “platform default key managers,” “KeyManagerFactory,” or the presentation label associated with the finding.","Whether the relevant context is used for client authentication, server authentication, or both; which key material is intended; and whether a changed manager configuration would select the same credentials. The cited sources do not answer these application-specific compatibility questions.","Which Java versions and providers must be supported. Although JDK 11 documentation identifies its TLS 1.3 introduction, that does not establish feature availability or defaults for all older JDKs or other providers."]}
